1
00:00:04,563 --> 00:00:07,065
[footsteps]

2
00:00:07,107 --> 00:00:11,403
[MAN 06A panting]

3
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:20,788
[MAN 06A continues panting]

4
00:00:20,830 --> 00:00:23,582
[snarling]

5
00:00:23,624 --> 00:00:27,169
[MAN 06A panting]

6
00:00:49,149 --> 00:00:51,569
[chuckles]

7
00:02:38,258 --> 00:02:40,344
It's a manmade wonder.

8
00:02:40,385 --> 00:02:42,513
I'm doing well. And you?

9
00:02:42,554 --> 00:02:45,182
I've seen a lot,
but nothing this impressive.

10
00:02:47,935 --> 00:02:49,269
Tch.

11
00:02:50,813 --> 00:02:53,398
[ENDERS] Why are there
so many dregs boarding?

12
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:56,777
They're sullying this majestic
vessel's maiden voyage.

13
00:02:56,819 --> 00:02:59,864
[FRED] They're passengers,
the same as you, sir.

14
00:03:00,823 --> 00:03:02,908
The passenger cabins
have different classes.

15
00:03:02,950 --> 00:03:05,744
And a decent number of them
are priced quite moderately

16
00:03:05,786 --> 00:03:09,707
I'm saying this ship is only fit
to be boarded by noblemen.

17
00:03:09,748 --> 00:03:13,251
Gaze upon it. I dare you
to tell me otherwise.

18
00:03:15,129 --> 00:03:16,964
[ENDERS]
She's the Noahtic.

19
00:03:17,006 --> 00:03:21,385
A ship truly worthy of
taking on the name "Noah."

20
00:03:21,426 --> 00:03:22,678
Be frank.

21
00:03:22,720 --> 00:03:24,722
Do you think a commoner
would be selected

22
00:03:24,763 --> 00:03:27,058
to construct the Ark,
saving all of humankind?

23
00:03:27,099 --> 00:03:28,266
Uh.

24
00:03:28,308 --> 00:03:30,644
[ENDERS] Noah was chosen by God!

25
00:03:30,686 --> 00:03:34,106
He obviously was a nobleman,
no different than me!

26
00:03:35,941 --> 00:03:37,860
[ENDERS]
Look around you.

27
00:03:37,902 --> 00:03:42,239
Notice the multitudes of God's
chosen eager to venture forth.

28
00:03:42,280 --> 00:03:44,575
Now, picture this.
Say by chance,

29
00:03:44,616 --> 00:03:47,619
a great flood were to hit
London while we were at sea.

30
00:03:47,661 --> 00:03:49,914
There'd be no substantial loss.

31
00:03:49,955 --> 00:03:52,041
Don't you agree?

32
00:03:52,083 --> 00:03:53,834
The only ones left
on this island

33
00:03:53,876 --> 00:03:55,586
would be the lowest of the low.

34
00:03:55,627 --> 00:03:57,922
If only they were
swallowed up by the waves,

35
00:03:57,963 --> 00:04:00,632
society'd have a chance.

36
00:04:00,674 --> 00:04:03,844
[LADY 06A gasps] Isn't that
Lord Enders over there?

37
00:04:03,886 --> 00:04:05,345
Of course, a man of his standing

38
00:04:05,387 --> 00:04:06,972
would book passage
on the Noahtic.

39
00:04:07,014 --> 00:04:09,225
[LADY 06C] It is such an honour
to make your acquaintance!

40
00:04:09,266 --> 00:04:12,352
What a relief, knowing refined
creatures will be aboard.

41
00:04:12,394 --> 00:04:14,646
[LADY 06A] Naturally,
we couldn't resist the urge

42
00:04:14,688 --> 00:04:16,690
to watch a once in
a lifetime performance!

43
00:04:16,732 --> 00:04:18,692
[LADY 06B] The first ballet
performed at sea!

44
00:04:18,734 --> 00:04:21,195
Missing such a monumental
event is unthinkable!

45
00:04:21,237 --> 00:04:22,780
[ENDERS laughs]

46
00:04:22,821 --> 00:04:25,616
What lovely minds to
match your lovely faces.

47
00:04:25,657 --> 00:04:28,326
Onward. Let's take
our rightful place.

48
00:04:28,368 --> 00:04:32,289
After all, the arc was built for
carrying noble blood like ours.

49
00:04:32,330 --> 00:04:34,833
[LADY 06A]
You lead the way, Lord Enders!

50
00:04:34,875 --> 00:04:36,501
I'm pleased to report
that the target

51
00:04:36,543 --> 00:04:38,503
has boarded at
the scheduled time.

52
00:04:42,466 --> 00:04:46,470
[WILLIAM] Well, let's
get this show started.

53
00:04:51,809 --> 00:04:54,937
I often reflect on the
inequity within this country.

54
00:04:54,979 --> 00:04:57,815
It has long
occupied my thoughts.

55
00:04:57,856 --> 00:04:59,942
And we're all keenly
aware of the cause:

56
00:04:59,984 --> 00:05:03,154
The broken class system, which
benefits a wealthy minority,

57
00:05:03,195 --> 00:05:05,614
whilst depriving everyone else.

58
00:05:05,656 --> 00:05:08,575
The temple must
come crashing down.

59
00:05:08,617 --> 00:05:10,119
We're in agreement.

60
00:05:10,161 --> 00:05:12,955
But who do we target
and how do we go about it?

61
00:05:12,997 --> 00:05:16,458
[WILLIAM] We can't change
the system overnight.

62
00:05:16,500 --> 00:05:21,005
However, something is achievable
in the immediate future.

63
00:05:21,046 --> 00:05:24,549
We can turn London into
a city overrun with crime,

64
00:05:24,591 --> 00:05:26,760
plunging it into
the depths of Hell.

65
00:05:26,802 --> 00:05:28,345
[BOTH gasp]

66
00:05:28,386 --> 00:05:29,888
[ALBERT chuckles]

67
00:05:29,930 --> 00:05:32,975
Intriguing, but I need
to hear more details.

68
00:05:33,017 --> 00:05:34,310
For starters, how does this help

69
00:05:34,351 --> 00:05:37,938
advance our objective of
improving life in this country?

70
00:05:37,980 --> 00:05:40,274
[WILLIAM] Crime is not
our ultimate objective;

71
00:05:40,316 --> 00:05:43,068
it's a means to
an end. That's all.

72
00:05:43,110 --> 00:05:45,237
As I stated, we cannot
change the system

73
00:05:45,279 --> 00:05:47,239
with a single decisive action.

74
00:05:47,281 --> 00:05:48,907
But with a well-placed stab,

75
00:05:48,949 --> 00:05:51,202
we could change
the minds of men.

76
00:05:51,243 --> 00:05:53,037
How so?

77
00:05:53,078 --> 00:05:55,580
Human beings have a
wide range of emotions

78
00:05:55,622 --> 00:05:58,209
and are quite often
willing to act upon them.

79
00:05:58,250 --> 00:05:59,793
But there's one event
that drives them

80
00:05:59,835 --> 00:06:01,837
to act above all else.

81
00:06:01,879 --> 00:06:04,882
It's called death.

82
00:06:04,923 --> 00:06:06,550
[BOTH grunt]

83
00:06:06,591 --> 00:06:11,596
I concur with you. Death,
above all else, is instrumental.

84
00:06:11,638 --> 00:06:15,851
Look, if me dying's gonna help
advance your plan in any way,

85
00:06:15,893 --> 00:06:17,853
well then, I'm your man.

86
00:06:17,895 --> 00:06:19,230
Same goes for you, right?

87
00:06:19,271 --> 00:06:20,856
[FRED]
Mm.

88
00:06:20,898 --> 00:06:23,150
[WILLIAM]
I guaranteed Londoners a show.

89
00:06:23,192 --> 00:06:27,571
And I have many deaths and
misdeeds tailor made for them.

90
00:06:27,612 --> 00:06:29,614
[WOMAN 06A gasps]

91
00:06:29,656 --> 00:06:36,080
[WILLIAM]
In short...

92
00:06:36,121 --> 00:06:38,456
[AUDIENCE shrieks]

93
00:06:38,498 --> 00:06:41,377
[WILLIAM] ...crime will
transform the city into a stage.

94
00:06:41,418 --> 00:06:44,880
And its citizens shall become
the audience, bearing witness.

95
00:06:44,922 --> 00:06:48,842
[ALBERT] You propose turning the
whole of London into a theatre?

96
00:06:48,884 --> 00:06:50,302
[WILLIAM]
Not only that,

97
00:06:50,344 --> 00:06:53,097
I've also chosen the
theme for our performances:

98
00:06:53,138 --> 00:06:56,600
Exposing the ills of
our society through murder.

99
00:06:56,641 --> 00:06:59,353
We'll work uncredited,
Choreographing the deaths

100
00:06:59,395 --> 00:07:01,730
and presenting them
in a dramatic fashion.

101
00:07:01,772 --> 00:07:03,357
The people of this country need

102
00:07:03,399 --> 00:07:06,693
an explosive display
to open their eyes.

103
00:07:06,735 --> 00:07:10,406
[MORAN] And I suspect we're the
ones pulling the trigger.

104
00:07:10,447 --> 00:07:12,950
Should prove to be interesting.

105
00:07:12,991 --> 00:07:15,327
[WILLIAM chuckles]

106
00:07:15,369 --> 00:07:16,578
[WILLIAM]
Very soon

107
00:07:16,620 --> 00:07:19,331
the time will come
for the curtain to rise.

108
00:07:19,373 --> 00:07:22,167
Depending on the success
of our forthcoming show,

109
00:07:22,209 --> 00:07:24,044
it could mark a
significant step towards

110
00:07:24,086 --> 00:07:27,131
the world we've
been striving for.

111
00:07:27,172 --> 00:07:28,673
But before that,

112
00:07:28,715 --> 00:07:32,136
I'll present our
first illustrious star.

113
00:07:35,597 --> 00:07:40,144
[WILLIAM] A recognizable name,
Count Blitz Enders.

114
00:07:45,399 --> 00:07:47,067
[ENDERS]
Step aside.

115
00:07:47,109 --> 00:07:49,027
[THOMAS MICHAELSON grunts]

116
00:07:49,069 --> 00:07:50,112
Hey!

117
00:07:50,154 --> 00:07:52,406
Who're you to board
ahead of everyone else?

118
00:07:52,448 --> 00:07:54,992
We were here first!
Get to the back of the line!

119
00:07:55,033 --> 00:07:56,243
Do you have any idea

120
00:07:56,285 --> 00:07:57,744
who you have the
privilege of addressing?

121
00:07:57,786 --> 00:07:59,121
[THOMAS MICHAELSON]
Huh?

122
00:07:59,163 --> 00:08:02,499
Lines were made for swine
like you, not lords and ladies.

123
00:08:02,540 --> 00:08:03,583
[THOMAS MICHAELSON]
What did you call me?

124
00:08:03,625 --> 00:08:06,170
[MORAN] Accept my
sincerest apologies, sir.

125
00:08:06,211 --> 00:08:08,255
Step right this way
if you would please.

126
00:08:08,297 --> 00:08:12,176
[ENDERS scoffs] It won't do any
good arguing with these people.

127
00:08:12,217 --> 00:08:14,428
If they choose to board
the boat like cattle,

128
00:08:14,470 --> 00:08:16,138
they ought to be
placed down in the hold

129
00:08:16,180 --> 00:08:17,348
like they livestock they are.

130
00:08:17,389 --> 00:08:18,807
Get back here!

131
00:08:25,189 --> 00:08:26,648
[STEWARD 06A]
The captain would like to extend

132
00:08:26,690 --> 00:08:29,109
a warm welcome
to all passengers.

133
00:08:29,151 --> 00:08:31,945
[CAPTAIN 06A] The Noahtic is now
beginning its pleasure cruise

134
00:08:31,987 --> 00:08:34,448
towards our destination
of Amsterdam.

135
00:08:34,490 --> 00:08:35,740
In addition, tomorrow,

136
00:08:35,782 --> 00:08:38,785
the world's first shipborne
ballet will be performed.

137
00:08:38,827 --> 00:08:40,120
We hope you all enjoy the show,

138
00:08:40,162 --> 00:08:42,498
as well as the remainder
of your voyage.

139
00:08:42,539 --> 00:08:44,708
[CROWD chatters]

140
00:08:49,587 --> 00:08:52,007
And how is our
"event" coming along?

141
00:08:52,049 --> 00:08:55,093
[WILLIAM chuckles]
Impatient as always, I see.

142
00:08:55,135 --> 00:08:57,637
The curtain will rise
once everyone's in place.

143
00:08:57,679 --> 00:08:59,890
[ALBERT]
Pity. Very well.

144
00:08:59,931 --> 00:09:02,100
While I eagerly await
the start of the show,

145
00:09:02,142 --> 00:09:05,312
I'll have to find some
way to entertain myself.

146
00:09:07,939 --> 00:09:08,982
[ENDERS]
Preposterous!

147
00:09:09,024 --> 00:09:11,235
You don't have a
reservation under my name?!

148
00:09:11,276 --> 00:09:12,777
That's simply impossible!

149
00:09:12,819 --> 00:09:14,196
I am terribly sorry,

150
00:09:14,238 --> 00:09:17,074
but unfortunately, there was a
slight mix-up with your table.

151
00:09:17,115 --> 00:09:18,616
What do you mean a mix-up?

152
00:09:18,658 --> 00:09:19,951
However, if you'd like,

153
00:09:19,993 --> 00:09:22,329
I could have a table prepared
for you at our restaurant

154
00:09:22,371 --> 00:09:23,205
located on the lower deck.

155
00:09:23,247 --> 00:09:24,373
So, in other words,

156
00:09:24,415 --> 00:09:26,041
you're saying I'm fit
to dine with the livestock

157
00:09:26,083 --> 00:09:28,335
while they stuff their gullets
in some mess hall below?

158
00:09:28,377 --> 00:09:29,211
Is that it?!

159
00:09:29,253 --> 00:09:31,088
I would never
insinuate that, sir.

160
00:09:31,129 --> 00:09:33,048
I belong to the
upper class, which means

161
00:09:33,090 --> 00:09:35,884
peasants don't even deserve
the honor of watching me eat!

162
00:09:35,926 --> 00:09:36,968
Are we clear?

163
00:09:37,010 --> 00:09:38,345
[WAITER 06A]
Y-Yes, sir.

164
00:09:38,387 --> 00:09:40,305
If you'll wait just one moment,
I'll have this sorted out.

165
00:09:40,347 --> 00:09:41,723
We'll find you seating at once.

166
00:09:41,765 --> 00:09:43,350
- -[splash]
- -[CHILD 6A gasps]

167
00:09:44,601 --> 00:09:46,061
[ENDERS growls]

168
00:09:46,103 --> 00:09:48,730
[MOTHER 06A]
I'm so sorry, Lord Enders!

169
00:09:48,772 --> 00:09:51,149
[ENDERS groans]

170
00:09:51,191 --> 00:09:53,735
If we so happen to
cross paths again,

171
00:09:53,777 --> 00:09:55,487
I swear, I'll murder you.

172
00:09:55,529 --> 00:09:57,739
[CHILD 06A yelps, cries]

173
00:09:57,781 --> 00:09:58,490
[CHILD 06A]
Mummy!

174
00:09:58,532 --> 00:10:01,034
Oh! What's the matter, honey?

175
00:10:01,076 --> 00:10:03,120
I can't think of any
reason why commoners

176
00:10:03,161 --> 00:10:04,621
would be this close
to a restaurant

177
00:10:04,662 --> 00:10:06,164
solely for first class.

178
00:10:06,206 --> 00:10:07,958
Apparently, the entire vessel

179
00:10:07,999 --> 00:10:10,168
is crawling with
all manner of vermin.

180
00:10:10,210 --> 00:10:12,837
[WILLIAM]
That was unfortunate.

181
00:10:12,879 --> 00:10:15,424
Might I offer you this, sir?

182
00:10:16,550 --> 00:10:18,594
Oh. Thank you kindly.

183
00:10:18,635 --> 00:10:22,389
[WILLIAM] My name is Moriarty.
William James Moriarty.

184
00:10:22,431 --> 00:10:25,183
Moriarty, huh?
I've heard the name before.

185
00:10:25,225 --> 00:10:26,768
You served in the army?

186
00:10:26,810 --> 00:10:29,229
[WILLIAM] You're thinking
of my older brother, Albert.

187
00:10:29,271 --> 00:10:30,730
He's also on board.

188
00:10:30,772 --> 00:10:32,107
I'll have to
introduce you later.

189
00:10:32,149 --> 00:10:36,278
[WAITER 06A] Lord Enders, we
have a table prepared for you.

190
00:10:36,320 --> 00:10:37,154
This way.

191
00:10:37,195 --> 00:10:40,073
Right. Till next time.

192
00:10:40,115 --> 00:10:42,700
I can't wait to see
what you mess up next.

193
00:10:42,742 --> 00:10:45,036
What sort of ship are
you running here?!

194
00:10:48,957 --> 00:10:51,168
I get 'em riled
up enough for you?

195
00:10:51,209 --> 00:10:53,337
[MORAN]
Yes. Well played.

196
00:10:53,378 --> 00:10:55,297
I trust you'll continue to
aggravate the lord

197
00:10:55,339 --> 00:10:58,175
for the remainder of our time
at sea, Mister Michaelson.

198
00:10:58,216 --> 00:10:59,968
Those were the terms
of the agreement

199
00:11:00,010 --> 00:11:01,803
and I ain't contesting 'em.

200
00:11:01,845 --> 00:11:03,721
But I just got one question.

201
00:11:03,763 --> 00:11:07,351
Why go through all the trouble
of pesterin' some ponce?

202
00:11:07,392 --> 00:11:08,393
Huh?

203
00:11:08,435 --> 00:11:10,521
It's not your place to inquire.

204
00:11:10,562 --> 00:11:13,148
That was also part of
our agreement, remember?

205
00:11:13,190 --> 00:11:14,858
[THOMAS MICHAELSON grunts]

206
00:11:14,899 --> 00:11:16,527
Long as I'm paid
what we agreed on,

207
00:11:16,568 --> 00:11:18,778
you won't get
no quarrel from me.

208
00:11:22,991 --> 00:11:25,785
[WILLIAM]
His name's Thomas Michaelson.

209
00:11:25,827 --> 00:11:27,454
A habitual burglar

210
00:11:27,496 --> 00:11:29,665
who's committed
a litany of crimes,

211
00:11:29,706 --> 00:11:34,044
including rape and even
murder when it suited him.

212
00:11:35,712 --> 00:11:38,756
[WILLIAM]
We will take his depraved life

213
00:11:38,798 --> 00:11:40,925
and make good use of it.

214
00:11:46,139 --> 00:11:48,808
[NOBLEMAN 06A] Lord Moriarty,
what a lovely surprise.

215
00:11:48,850 --> 00:11:51,311
Didn't imagine
I'd be running into you,

216
00:11:51,353 --> 00:11:53,230
and here, of all places.

217
00:11:53,271 --> 00:11:55,357
If this were merely
a cruise, I confess,

218
00:11:55,399 --> 00:11:57,401
I would have
very little interest.

219
00:11:57,442 --> 00:12:00,696
But I heard a magnificent
spectacle was taking place.

220
00:12:00,737 --> 00:12:03,574
Might you be referring to the
world's first shipborne ballet?

221
00:12:03,615 --> 00:12:04,991
[ALBERT chuckles]

222
00:12:05,033 --> 00:12:06,577
[NOBLEMAN 06A]
Why that's the very reason

223
00:12:06,618 --> 00:12:08,119
I bought a ticket myself.

224
00:12:08,161 --> 00:12:09,538
[ALBERT]
You don't say.

225
00:12:09,580 --> 00:12:14,585
As a ballet aficionado, I'm sure
it was impossible to resist.

226
00:12:14,626 --> 00:12:16,545
You have deduced correctly.

227
00:12:16,587 --> 00:12:19,423
I'll admit, the moment
I stepped foot on the ship,

228
00:12:19,464 --> 00:12:22,967
I haven't been able to stop
thinking about the performance.

229
00:12:23,009 --> 00:12:27,931
[ALBERT] Yes. I as well. I'm
sure whatever they have prepared

230
00:12:27,972 --> 00:12:30,267
will leave us breathless.

231
00:12:30,308 --> 00:12:31,893
[ENDERS growls]

232
00:12:34,812 --> 00:12:36,022
[knocking]

233
00:12:36,064 --> 00:12:40,444
[FRED] Blitz Enders.
I have a telegram for you, sir.

234
00:12:40,485 --> 00:12:41,819
[ENDERS]
Hm?

235
00:12:45,323 --> 00:12:47,492
[WILLIAM]
Should be any moment now

236
00:12:47,534 --> 00:12:51,538
I expect our lead will be
receiving some unfortunate news.

237
00:12:51,580 --> 00:12:53,081
[grunts]

238
00:13:07,053 --> 00:13:08,263
[LADY 06D]
Amazing!

239
00:13:08,305 --> 00:13:10,890
How did you know that
gentleman was in the military?

240
00:13:10,932 --> 00:13:12,892
[LADY 06E] Surely, he mentioned
it to you beforehand!

241
00:13:12,934 --> 00:13:15,228
[LADY 06F]
Hmm... What about that man?

242
00:13:15,270 --> 00:13:18,940
Can you guess his profession?

243
00:13:18,982 --> 00:13:21,025
[HOLMES]
Hmm.

244
00:13:21,067 --> 00:13:22,277
Yeah, I got it.

245
00:13:22,319 --> 00:13:23,861
You barely looked!

246
00:13:23,903 --> 00:13:25,405
[LADY 06E]
If you guess correctly,

247
00:13:25,447 --> 00:13:28,074
that'll be ten times in a row!

248
00:13:28,116 --> 00:13:29,826
Beg your pardon, sir.

249
00:13:29,867 --> 00:13:31,787
It seems you've become
an unwilling participant

250
00:13:31,828 --> 00:13:32,912
in our bet.

251
00:13:32,954 --> 00:13:34,372
A bet? Really?

252
00:13:34,414 --> 00:13:35,624
[LADY 06F]
Yes.

253
00:13:35,666 --> 00:13:37,834
It simply entails us standing
here, people watching,

254
00:13:37,875 --> 00:13:39,919
while he takes a guess
as to their occupations.

255
00:13:39,961 --> 00:13:41,879
Go on and tell us quickly!

256
00:13:41,921 --> 00:13:44,257
What does that
gentleman do for a living?

257
00:13:44,299 --> 00:13:47,594
[HOLMES] Regarding
the man's line of work,

258
00:13:47,636 --> 00:13:49,429
it's rather elementary.

259
00:13:51,306 --> 00:13:53,475
He's a mathematician.

260
00:14:01,358 --> 00:14:03,318
It's rather elementary.

261
00:14:04,319 --> 00:14:06,822
He's a mathematician.

262
00:14:06,863 --> 00:14:08,573
[LADY 06E]
Now explain it step-by-step.

263
00:14:08,615 --> 00:14:11,409
[LADY 06F]
Yes! Please, indulge us!

264
00:14:11,451 --> 00:14:12,703
[HOLMES]
First,

265
00:14:12,744 --> 00:14:16,080
there's no denying it's
a hell of a spiral staircase.

266
00:14:16,122 --> 00:14:18,834
I can see why you
were admiring it.

267
00:14:18,875 --> 00:14:22,295
Ah. Which element, though?

268
00:14:22,337 --> 00:14:24,798
What makes you so sure
I'm a mathematician?

269
00:14:24,840 --> 00:14:27,008
Hmm? It's simple.

270
00:14:27,050 --> 00:14:28,176
Whilst I was observing,

271
00:14:28,218 --> 00:14:30,303
I noticed how long
you stood in place,

272
00:14:30,345 --> 00:14:33,515
distance, and the
angle you chose.

273
00:14:33,557 --> 00:14:35,433
I'll continue.

274
00:14:35,475 --> 00:14:37,561
Truth can be found
through simple deductions

275
00:14:37,602 --> 00:14:40,104
based on knowledge and behavior.

276
00:14:40,146 --> 00:14:42,023
Despite clearly being
absorbed in thought

277
00:14:42,065 --> 00:14:43,400
while walking across the lounge,

278
00:14:43,441 --> 00:14:47,320
your concentration was broken
once you reached the stairs.

279
00:14:47,362 --> 00:14:48,697
Dare I say,

280
00:14:48,739 --> 00:14:50,699
almost as if the sight had
caused your mind

281
00:14:50,741 --> 00:14:52,409
to come to momentary halt

282
00:14:52,450 --> 00:14:55,203
and all other
matters dissipated.

283
00:14:55,245 --> 00:14:56,663
There's no denying the staircase

284
00:14:56,705 --> 00:14:59,123
is beautifully adorned
and worth admiring.

285
00:14:59,165 --> 00:15:00,834
But where was your attention?

286
00:15:00,876 --> 00:15:01,793
You didn't step closer to

287
00:15:01,835 --> 00:15:04,546
appreciate the
intricate ornamentation.

288
00:15:04,588 --> 00:15:06,798
Instead, you took
a step backwards,

289
00:15:06,840 --> 00:15:10,301
allowing you to observe
the staircase as a whole.

290
00:15:10,343 --> 00:15:13,012
And what profession
matches this behavior?

291
00:15:13,054 --> 00:15:15,014
An appreciation for
the "construction"

292
00:15:15,056 --> 00:15:17,517
and not "cosmetic beauty"?

293
00:15:17,559 --> 00:15:20,812
An architect?
Or a physicist, perhaps?

294
00:15:20,854 --> 00:15:22,522
On the contrary...

295
00:15:22,564 --> 00:15:25,692
His piercing gaze was
not directed at the column

296
00:15:25,734 --> 00:15:27,235
because his interest
wasn't in the manner

297
00:15:27,277 --> 00:15:29,195
in which the stairs
were supported.

298
00:15:29,237 --> 00:15:30,781
So, the question becomes

299
00:15:30,822 --> 00:15:34,951
what was he looking at that
caused him to step backwards?

300
00:15:34,992 --> 00:15:39,205
He wanted the grand scope.
To seek confirmation.

301
00:15:39,247 --> 00:15:41,583
The curve of the staircase
and whether or not

302
00:15:41,625 --> 00:15:44,252
it adhered to
a certain principle:

303
00:15:44,294 --> 00:15:46,337
The divine proportion.

304
00:15:46,379 --> 00:15:49,257
By which I mean
the golden ratio.

305
00:15:51,593 --> 00:15:55,096
You're correct.
What a keen observer you are.

306
00:15:55,138 --> 00:15:58,141
Whilst the logic of your
argument was a trifle forced,

307
00:15:58,182 --> 00:16:01,686
I am a professor. I teach
mathematics at university.

308
00:16:01,728 --> 00:16:02,813
Then you mean...

309
00:16:02,854 --> 00:16:05,106
That makes ten in a row!

310
00:16:05,148 --> 00:16:09,486
Now then, it would be remiss
of me not to return the favour.

311
00:16:09,527 --> 00:16:10,779
Uh.

312
00:16:10,821 --> 00:16:12,948
[WILLIAM]
Where to start?

313
00:16:12,989 --> 00:16:15,784
You play the strings,
violin specifically,

314
00:16:15,826 --> 00:16:18,369
but you're not
a professional musician.

315
00:16:18,411 --> 00:16:20,622
Additionally, you're adept
in physical activities,

316
00:16:20,664 --> 00:16:21,998
such as boxing.

317
00:16:22,039 --> 00:16:26,628
You have an avid interest in
scientific experiments and...

318
00:16:26,670 --> 00:16:28,338
[WILLIAM sniffs]

319
00:16:28,379 --> 00:16:31,716
Do I detect the slightest hint
of a chemical dependency?

320
00:16:31,758 --> 00:16:33,384
[HOLMES grunts]

321
00:16:35,094 --> 00:16:36,721
Even though you give
me the impression

322
00:16:36,763 --> 00:16:38,723
of being an Oxbridge
educated man,

323
00:16:38,765 --> 00:16:40,684
I find your dialect
to be more in the vein

324
00:16:40,725 --> 00:16:42,185
of the working class.

325
00:16:42,226 --> 00:16:43,478
As for the why,

326
00:16:43,520 --> 00:16:46,648
no doubt it's because you
take pride in your ancestry,

327
00:16:46,690 --> 00:16:49,609
specifically,
your mother's side.

328
00:16:49,651 --> 00:16:51,110
Well?

329
00:16:52,529 --> 00:16:54,322
How'd you ever guess?

330
00:16:54,364 --> 00:16:57,033
I didn't.

331
00:16:57,074 --> 00:16:59,911
Everything I said was a fact.

332
00:17:01,830 --> 00:17:07,711
[laughs] What an
intriguing chap!

333
00:17:07,752 --> 00:17:09,671
Do you think so?

334
00:17:09,713 --> 00:17:12,507
Come! There are more
people on the upper deck.

335
00:17:12,549 --> 00:17:13,842
Still not sated?

336
00:17:13,884 --> 00:17:15,677
[LADY 06E]
Hardly, we're going to have to

337
00:17:15,719 --> 00:17:18,054
make you keep going
until you make a mistake!

338
00:17:18,095 --> 00:17:20,515
I'm at your mercy.

339
00:17:20,557 --> 00:17:23,977
Oh, professor. See you around.

340
00:17:25,687 --> 00:17:27,230
[LADY 06D]
It never gets old.

341
00:17:27,271 --> 00:17:28,982
Anything wrong?

342
00:17:29,024 --> 00:17:30,191
No.

343
00:17:30,233 --> 00:17:34,111
He shouldn't pose any
great hindrance to our plan.

344
00:17:34,153 --> 00:17:35,112
And you?

345
00:17:35,154 --> 00:17:36,698
Everything's on schedule.

346
00:17:36,740 --> 00:17:38,324
The target's on
the verge of breaking.

347
00:17:38,366 --> 00:17:41,995
Good. Continue
chipping away at him.

348
00:17:42,037 --> 00:17:44,706
At this rate, it shouldn't
take much longer.

349
00:17:44,748 --> 00:17:47,250
His endurance is all
but depleted.

350
00:17:59,262 --> 00:18:02,181
[LADY 06A] He looks grim.
What could be troubling him?

351
00:18:02,223 --> 00:18:03,224
[LADY 06C]
From what I understand,

352
00:18:03,266 --> 00:18:05,184
he's had a streak of bad luck.

353
00:18:05,226 --> 00:18:06,436
More than that.

354
00:18:06,477 --> 00:18:08,563
He received a telegram
saying his cottage burnt down

355
00:18:08,605 --> 00:18:10,774
and there was an accident
at his colliery.

356
00:18:10,815 --> 00:18:13,693
I can't imagine the poor man
would want any company.

357
00:18:13,735 --> 00:18:15,695
Probably best to leave him be.

358
00:18:15,737 --> 00:18:17,697
You're right.

359
00:18:19,657 --> 00:18:23,578
[ENDERS] It doesn't make sense.
Why is this happening?

360
00:18:23,620 --> 00:18:26,664
Ever since I've boarded,
I've met with misfortune

361
00:18:26,706 --> 00:18:28,458
as though there's
some conspiracy

362
00:18:28,499 --> 00:18:30,919
to ruin and humiliate me!

363
00:18:30,961 --> 00:18:33,713
Damn it all!
I can't take much more!

364
00:18:33,755 --> 00:18:37,801
What I wouldn't give for the
comfort of my hunting grounds!

365
00:18:37,842 --> 00:18:39,094
No.

366
00:18:39,135 --> 00:18:43,681
Wait. Why should they
be limited to the woods?

367
00:18:50,063 --> 00:18:52,649
[ENDERS]
This is, after all, Noah's Ark.

368
00:18:52,690 --> 00:18:54,776
And the nobility
are the only passengers

369
00:18:54,818 --> 00:18:56,903
which could be considered human.

370
00:18:56,945 --> 00:18:59,739
There is certainly no shortage
of dumb beasts aboard

371
00:18:59,781 --> 00:19:05,703
I can select from to be my prey.

372
00:19:05,745 --> 00:19:08,957
[chuckles]

373
00:19:12,460 --> 00:19:14,504
[THOMAS MICHAELSON] You're
absolutely sure about this?

374
00:19:14,545 --> 00:19:15,588
[ENDERS]
While there's nothing I can do

375
00:19:15,630 --> 00:19:17,841
to take back the truly
dreadful things I said

376
00:19:17,882 --> 00:19:19,258
while we were boarding,

377
00:19:19,300 --> 00:19:21,636
I will do my utmost
to apologize.

378
00:19:21,678 --> 00:19:24,806
Hah! You aristocrats know
how to correct a mistake.

379
00:19:24,848 --> 00:19:26,557
[ENDERS]
Check the inner stateroom.

380
00:19:26,599 --> 00:19:27,934
[THOMAS MICHAELSON]
They pull out all the stops

381
00:19:27,976 --> 00:19:30,186
for first-class
cabins, don't they?

382
00:19:32,897 --> 00:19:35,358
[gasps] Lord have mercy!

383
00:19:35,399 --> 00:19:37,068
Which of you to start with?

384
00:19:37,110 --> 00:19:39,236
It's so hard to choose.

385
00:19:39,278 --> 00:19:41,197
[THOMAS MICHAELSON] This is all
too perfect.

386
00:19:41,238 --> 00:19:42,866
I'll pretend
I'm good and soused,

387
00:19:42,907 --> 00:19:44,951
make him throw a fit
and apologize again.

388
00:19:44,993 --> 00:19:47,078
Won't take long to
wheedle all the spirits

389
00:19:47,120 --> 00:19:50,081
out of this rich
twit's liquor cabinet.

390
00:19:50,123 --> 00:19:51,582
[THOMAS MICHAELSON]
Between you and me,

391
00:19:51,624 --> 00:19:54,794
I always can pick
a winner. Heh, heh.

392
00:19:54,836 --> 00:19:56,087
This should do.

393
00:19:56,129 --> 00:19:58,089
I can any have
any bottle I want?

394
00:19:58,131 --> 00:20:00,008
Yes,

395
00:20:00,050 --> 00:20:02,635
on one condition:

396
00:20:02,677 --> 00:20:04,470
If you somehow survive!

397
00:20:04,512 --> 00:20:08,808
- -[thud]
- -[THOMAS MICHAELSON groans]

398
00:20:08,850 --> 00:20:10,601
You're mad!

399
00:20:10,643 --> 00:20:12,979
[ENDERS cackles]
There really is no difference

400
00:20:13,021 --> 00:20:14,814
between you and a swine!

401
00:20:14,856 --> 00:20:19,152
Even your pleas before death
sound like a squealing animal's!

402
00:20:19,194 --> 00:20:21,404
[THOMAS MICHAELSON]
No! Don't!

403
00:20:22,613 --> 00:20:25,282
- -[ENDERS grunts]
- -[THOMAS MICHAELSON screams]

404
00:20:35,585 --> 00:20:38,088
What a fine evening
this has been.

405
00:20:39,547 --> 00:20:41,841
There really is a
different zest to killing

406
00:20:41,883 --> 00:20:43,384
using your own hands.

407
00:20:43,426 --> 00:20:46,303
A gun can be so
clinical and unfeeling.

408
00:20:46,345 --> 00:20:49,265
I'll have to implement it into
my hunting regime.

409
00:20:54,270 --> 00:20:56,106
[MORAN]
Pardon,

410
00:20:56,147 --> 00:20:59,734
but that special cut of meat
you ordered is ready, sir.

411
00:21:02,112 --> 00:21:04,572
Also, the room has been
left unlocked for you.

412
00:21:04,614 --> 00:21:06,908
So whenever you feel inclined...

413
00:21:06,950 --> 00:21:08,785
Thank you kindly.

414
00:21:15,208 --> 00:21:16,667
[ENDERS grunts, chuckles]

415
00:21:16,709 --> 00:21:18,294
[knocking]

416
00:21:18,335 --> 00:21:19,254
[WILLIAM]
Lord Enders?

417
00:21:19,295 --> 00:21:20,880
[gasps]

418
00:21:20,922 --> 00:21:22,465
[WILLIAM] It's Moriarty.

419
00:21:22,506 --> 00:21:25,218
Forgive me, but one of
the ladies informed me

420
00:21:25,260 --> 00:21:27,470
that she heard a shout
coming from your room.

421
00:21:27,511 --> 00:21:29,139
Are you not feeling well?

422
00:21:29,180 --> 00:21:32,100
I was terribly concerned
and wanted to check on you.

423
00:21:32,142 --> 00:21:34,018
[ENDERS]
Not Moriarty!

424
00:21:34,060 --> 00:21:36,604
This is bad! What do I do?

425
00:21:36,646 --> 00:21:39,649
If he finds the carcass,
I'm finished!

426
00:21:39,690 --> 00:21:42,110
[WILLIAM grunts]
The door's opened.

427
00:21:42,152 --> 00:21:43,444
I'm letting myself in.

428
00:21:43,486 --> 00:21:46,114
[ENDERS] But I distinctly
remember locking it behind me!

429
00:21:46,156 --> 00:21:48,032
[WILLIAM]
Lord Enders? Are you in here?

430
00:21:48,074 --> 00:21:49,826
[ENDERS] Wait. I'm just--
Just a moment!

431
00:21:49,867 --> 00:21:51,410
Oh.

432
00:21:52,411 --> 00:21:56,540
[ENDERS gasping]

433
00:21:59,752 --> 00:22:04,132
[WILLIAM] I must say...
this is quite serious.

434
00:23:35,014 --> 00:23:37,600
[HOLMES] Next time
on Moriarty the Patriot  .

435
00:23:37,641 --> 00:23:40,353
"The Noahtic Act Two."
